From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artyom Shalkhakov <artyom.shalkhakov@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0500
Subject: [PATCH] Move the attribute subtree when node adoption fails

-[NSXMLElement addAttribute:] moves the attribute into the element with
xmlDOMWrapAdoptNode(), ignores the result, and then frees the private
document the attribute came from. When the adoption fails, the
attribute's value nodes are left pointing at that freed document, and
the next thing to walk the attribute -- a later -detach, or the
parent's dealloc -- reads freed memory. The symptom is a segfault
inside xmlDictOwns(), reached through adoptString -> setTreeDoc ->
-[NSXMLNode detach].

The adoption fails for an attribute whose namespace has no href.
-[NSXMLNode setName:] with a prefix it cannot resolve -- an attribute
built before its element is in a document, so the prefix is not
declared yet -- makes a placeholder xmlNs with no href and a private
xmlDoc to hold it. libxml2 refuses to adopt a namespace without an
href, after it has set attr->doc and before it walks the value nodes,
and returns -1.

Every libxml2 from 2.9 to 2.13 fails the adoption the same way. The
dangling pointer is created deterministically; only whether reading it
crashes depends on what the allocator has since put in the freed chunk,
and on how the library was built. A gnustep-base compiled against
libxml2 2.12 or newer uses its own updateTreeDocManually() when the
element is later inserted, which rewrites every pointer under the
element and so repairs the attribute by accident; against 2.9.x it
calls xmlDOMWrapAdoptNode() again, whose source-document sanity check
skips exactly the node that needs fixing. That is why the crash looks
like a platform difference rather than a bug.

Check the result of xmlDOMWrapAdoptNode() and, when it fails, move the
attribute subtree with xmlSetTreeDoc() before the private document is
freed. The private document has no dictionary, so it owns none of the
strings and a plain pointer update is enough.

gnustep-base's own NSXML suites, 524 tests, pass with the change on
libxml2 2.9.14 and 2.13.8.

A reproduction program accompanies this patch rather than a test in
Tests/base: seeing the dangling pointer means reading the libxml2 node
behind the wrapper, which needs the libxml2 headers the test harness
does not pass. It exits 1 on an unpatched library and 0 with the
change, and shows the invalid read under valgrind.
---
diff --git a/Source/NSXMLElement.m b/Source/NSXMLElement.m
index 7484209..c1833aa 100644
--- a/Source/NSXMLElement.m
+++ b/Source/NSXMLElement.m
@@ -324,8 +324,23 @@ GS_PRIVATE_INTERNAL(NSXMLElement)
         }
 
 #if LIBXML_VERSION >= 20620
-      xmlDOMWrapAdoptNode(NULL, attr->doc, (xmlNodePtr)attr, 
-                          theNode->doc, theNode, 0);
+      if (xmlDOMWrapAdoptNode(NULL, attr->doc, (xmlNodePtr)attr,
+        theNode->doc, theNode, 0) != 0)
+        {
+          /* libxml2 refuses to adopt an attribute whose namespace has no
+           * href (the placeholder -setName: makes for a prefix that is not
+           * declared yet, to be resolved once the element is placed), and
+           * xmlDOMWrapAdoptAttr fails only after it has moved the attribute
+           * itself and before it walks the value nodes.  Those are left
+           * pointing at the private document freed below, so the next
+           * thing to walk the attribute (a later -detach, or the parent's
+           * dealloc) reads freed memory.  Move the whole subtree ourselves
+           * instead.  The private document has no dictionary, so it owns
+           * none of the strings and a plain pointer update is enough.
+           */
+          attr->doc = tmp;
+          xmlSetTreeDoc((xmlNodePtr)attr, theNode->doc);
+        }
 #else
       xmlSetTreeDoc((xmlNodePtr)attr, theNode->doc);
 #endif
